{
  "concept": "The line (adjoint/edge) graph transformation: every edge of the original graph becomes a node, and two such nodes are connected whenever the original edges shared an endpoint.",
  "figureType": "network_diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Three panel labels: 'a)', 'b)', 'c)' in the upper-left of each panel",
    "Panel titles: 'Original graph' (a) and 'Edge graph' (c)",
    "Panel a: six orange circular nodes with black outlines labeled 1,2,3,4,5,6",
    "Panel a edges (thin black, no arrowheads): 1-3, 2-3, 2-5, 1-5, 1-4, 4-5, 1-6, 4-6",
    "Panel b: the same six orange nodes, plus eight teal circular nodes placed at the midpoint of each original edge, labeled 2/3, 1/3, 2/5, 1/5, 1/4, 4/5, 1/6, 4/6",
    "Panel b: original black edges now each split into two segments passing through the new teal node",
    "Panel c: only the eight teal nodes, laid out in roughly the same relative positions, connected by black edges wherever the two original edges shared an endpoint (15 edges total)",
    "Grey arrow between panels a and b with caption 'Add new node at each original edge'",
    "Grey arrow between panels b and c with caption 'Connect new nodes if original edges shared node'",
    "Color key implicit: orange = original vertices, teal (#a0d9d3) = edge-nodes",
    "Highlight styling used by interactions: selected original edge in bold accent color, corresponding teal node ringed, generated line-graph edges drawn in the same accent color",
    "Optional readout text showing the shared endpoint and the count of connections generated by a selected edge"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "buildPhase",
      "type": "state",
      "label": "Construction step",
      "states": ["original", "placeNodes", "connectShared", "edgeGraph"],
      "default": "original",
      "teaches": "The transformation is two deliberate operations: first every edge is promoted to a vertex, then adjacency is inherited from shared endpoints."
    },
    {
      "id": "selectEdge",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "Each original edge maps to exactly one node of the line graph, and its degree there equals the number of other edges touching its two endpoints."
    },
    {
      "id": "viewMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Show",
      "options": ["Original", "Edge graph", "Both"],
      "default": "Both",
      "teaches": "Comparing the two structures directly reveals that a low-degree original graph can produce a much denser line graph."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The original graph",
      "narration": "Six orange vertices joined by eight undirected edges. Note the hub: vertex 1 touches four of the eight edges.",
      "state": { "buildPhase": "original", "viewMode": "Original" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Promote every edge to a node",
      "narration": "A teal node is dropped onto each original edge and named by its endpoints, so 1-3 becomes '1/3'. Eight edges give exactly eight new nodes.",
      "state": { "buildPhase": "placeNodes", "viewMode": "Both" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Inherit adjacency from shared endpoints",
      "narration": "Two teal nodes get connected whenever their original edges met at a vertex. Every original vertex of degree d turns into a fully connected clique of d teal nodes.",
      "state": { "buildPhase": "connectShared", "viewMode": "Both" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Read off the edge graph",
      "narration": "Dropping the orange scaffolding leaves the line graph: 8 nodes and 15 edges. Click any original edge to see which teal node it became and which clique it belongs to.",
      "state": { "buildPhase": "edgeGraph", "viewMode": "Edge graph" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Each vertex of degree d in the original graph becomes a complete clique of d nodes in the line graph — adjacency of edges replaces adjacency of vertices, which is why edge-centric GNNs can operate on this dual structure.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Three horizontal panels on white. Panel a (left): six orange (#d2714d) circles with black outlines labeled 1-6, arranged as in the figure — 3 top, 2 upper-left, 1 right-of-center, 5 far left, 4 lower-left, 6 lower-right; edges 1-3, 2-3, 2-5, 1-5, 1-4, 4-5, 1-6, 4-6 as thin plain black lines. Panel b (middle): same layout with a teal (#a0d9d3) circle sitting at the midpoint of each edge, labeled with the endpoint pair (2/3, 1/3, 2/5, 1/5, 1/4, 4/5, 1/6, 4/6); black lines now run vertex-to-teal-node-to-vertex. Panel c (right): only the eight teal nodes, keeping their approximate relative positions, densely interconnected (15 edges) with the tightest cluster around 1/5, 1/4, 1/3, 1/6 because vertex 1 has degree four. Two thick grey right-pointing arrows sit below the gaps between panels with two-line captions 'Add new node at each original edge' and 'Connect new nodes if original edges shared node'. Panel letters a), b), c) in the top-left of each panel in large light text; titles 'Original graph' and 'Edge graph' centered above panels a and c. Interaction highlights should use a single warm accent (e.g. gold/amber) for the selected edge, its teal counterpart node, and the edges it generates, dimming everything else to light grey."
}

